{"entries":{"chapter-74-study-coronary-heart-disease-and-dyslipidemia|Chapter 74 Study: Coronary Heart Disease and Dyslipidemia":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Coronary disease spans subclinical atherosclerosis, stable ischemia and acute coronary syndromes; ischemia reflects oxygen supply inadequate for myocardial demand (p1133).":["u3"],"copy|Typical angina remains the most common presentation regardless of age; dyspnea, fatigue or epigastric discomfort can delay recognition (p1133).":["u4"],"copy|More than 80% of US coronary deaths occur after 65. High baseline risk makes appropriate treatment consequential (p1133).":["u5"],"copy|New, progressive or refractory symptoms need expedited, sometimes inpatient evaluation. Assess symptoms and risk factors rather than dismissing complaints because of age (p1137).":["u6"],"copy|Functional testing remains useful; modified exercise or pharmacologic stress protocols can accommodate limitations (p1133).":["u7"],"copy|After MI, assess ventricular function and coronary severity. For NSTEMI, invasive assessment versus an ischemia-driven approach depends on risk and clinical context (p1143).":["u8"],"copy|Aspirin has strong secondary-prevention evidence; the chapter favors 81 mg daily in older adults. Higher doses increase bleeding without clear additional efficacy (p1139–1140).":["u9"],"copy|Primary prevention is different: ASPREE did not improve disability-free survival in healthy older adults and increased major hemorrhage (p1140).":["u10"],"copy|In adults at least 75 with LDL-C 70–189 mg/dL, moderate-intensity statin therapy may be reasonable; frailty, functional decline and limited life expectancy can limit benefit (p1141).":["u11"],"copy|ACE inhibitors are considered with coronary disease plus ventricular dysfunction, diabetes or hypertension; ARBs can substitute for ACE-inhibitor intolerance. Monitor creatinine and potassium (p1143).":["u12"],"copy|HOPE studied ramipril 10 mg daily in coronary disease or high-risk patients; this is the trial regimen, not a universal starting dose (p1143).":["u13"],"copy|Calculate creatinine clearance in ACS patients at least 75, and reassess as renal function changes; polypharmacy and altered drug responses increase harm (p1147).":["u14"],"copy|Do not transfer aspirin's secondary-prevention benefit directly to healthy older adults (p1139–1140).":["u15"],"copy|The chapter does not recommend oral anticoagulation solely for acute or chronic coronary disease without another indication: reduced ischemic events can be offset by bleeding (p1140).":["u16"],"copy|Primary-prevention statin evidence is limited in older adults; risk calculators derived only through age 79 perform suboptimally in older populations (p1141).":["u17"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u18"],"figure-74-5-book-image|Figure 74-5 — book image":["u19"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 74-5, p.1139":["u20","u34"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u21"],"copy|2024-May Q3":["u22","u32"],"copy|2024-May Q88":["u23"],"copy|CHAPTER 74s / HAZZARD":["u24"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 74 - Coronary Heart Disease and Dyslipidemia; the book and the full chapter are the reference; additional current-practice valve reading: ESC/EACTS 2025 and ACC/AHA 2020 with corrections.":["u25"],"copy|Disease & presentation":["u26"],"copy|Assessment":["u27"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u28"],"copy|Exam traps":["u29"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u30"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u31"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u33"],"copy|Five past-exam questions cover stress testing, antithrombotic treatment and discharge therapy after infarction.":["u33"],"copy|Hazzard describes a prasugrel boxed warning for age ≥75, prior stroke or low BMI because bleeding risk is increased.":["u180"],"copy|Ticagrelor has a different risk profile and still requires a bleeding-risk assessment.":["u93"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The US label contraindicates prior stroke/TIA and generally advises against use at age ≥75, with possible consideration for diabetes or prior MI.":["u181"],"copy|Low body weight (<60 kg) is a separate bleeding-risk factor.":["u182"],"copy|Prasugrel prescribing information, boxed warning and §4.2.":["u183"],"copy|The estimated at least 2.5 years to prevent one major event per 100 treated came from trials in adults aged 50–75.":["u132"]},"maxId":183,"sequenceOffsets":[[184,-149],[239,-145],[277,-144],[324,0]]}
